Q: Liquid Glass toolbar animation only plays when switching state rapidly A: <p>If you can move the <code>.toolbar</code> modifier to the <code>ZStack</code> then the solution described in my answer to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79683600/20386264">Liquid Glass Animation for resizing a collection of controls</a> can be used. This involves using a <code>ToolbarItemGroup</code> to contain the items:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>ZStack {
    switch tab {
    case .tab1:
        Text(&quot;Tab 1&quot;)

    case .tab2:
        Text(&quot;Tab 2&quot;)
    }
}
.toolbar {
    switch tab {
    case .tab1:
        ToolbarItemGroup(placement: .topBarLeading) {
            Button(&quot;Tab 1 Button&quot;, systemImage: &quot;person&quot;) { }
        }
    case .tab2:
        ToolbarItemGroup(placement: .topBarLeading) {
            Button(&quot;Tab 2 Button&quot;, systemImage: &quot;plus&quot;) { }
            Button(&quot;Tab 2 Button&quot;, systemImage: &quot;trash&quot;) { }
        }
    }
}
.animation(.default, value: tab)
// + other modifiers, as before
</code></pre>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/wiBJpEQY.gif" alt="Animation" /></p>

Q: How to update an existing csv file with Powershell? A: <p>I propose this solution, in use by combining [GUID]:[guid]::NewGuid().ToString(), then StreamReader and StreamWriter</p>
<pre><code>process{       
    #Import  B.csv
    $oldElementsCsv=Import-Csv $env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\Archi\B.csv -Delimiter ','
    #get Jobstream in A.csv and check in present in A.csv
    $reader='.\Archi\A.csv'
    $NewExtract_AGRe_TWS_ALL_20200925Jobstream=  New-Object IO.StreamReader ($reader)
    #Output progress:
    $path=&quot;.\Archi\A.csv&quot;
    $writer= New-Object  System.IO.StreamWriter ($path)
    $writer.WriteLine(($NewExtract_AGRe_TWS_ALL_20200925Jobstream.ReadLine()))
    $output= @()
    while ($null -ne ($line =$NewExtract_AGRe_TWS_ALL_20200925Jobstream.ReadLine()) ){
        $fields=$line -split ','
        ForEach ( $row in  $fields){
            $objMatch = $oldElementsCsv | Where-Object { $_.Name -ne $row.Jobstream} 
            if($objMatch){
                $output += New-Object PsObject -Property @{
                    ID=[guid]::NewGuid().ToString(); Type=&quot;N/A&quot; ; Name= $row.Jobstream; Documentation= $row.&quot;Job Description&quot;
                } 

                Write-Warning &quot;Found new Jobstream : $($row.&quot;Jobstream&quot;)&quot;
                Write-Warning &quot;Found new Jobstream Description : $($row.&quot;Job Description&quot;)&quot;
                $writer.WriteLine(($output -join ','))
                #$writer.Close()
            }
            else {
                $output += New-Object PsObject -Property @{ID= $_.ID; Type=$_Type; Name= $_Name; Documentation= $_.Documentation} 
                Write-Warning &quot;Found same value  for Name and  Jobstream  columns: $($_Name)&quot;
                Write-Warning &quot;Found same value for  new Jobstream Description and Documentation columns: $($_.Documentation)&quot;
                $writer.WriteLine(($output -join ','))
                #$writer.Close()
            }
        } 
    }

    $output | Export-Csv &quot;$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\Archi\B.csv&quot; -NoTypeInformation -Encoding UTF8 
    $NewExtract_AGRe_TWS_ALL_20200925Jobstream.Dispose()
    $NewExtract_AGRe_TWS_ALL_20200925Jobstream.Close()
    $writer.Dispose()
    $writer.Close()
    #open new File:
    notepad $env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\Archi\B.csv
}
</code></pre>
